\details{
The untreated outcome of unit i at time t is
\verb{y_it = x_it'b + mu + alpha_i + xi_t + lambda_i'f_t + e_it}, the
interactive fixed effects model of Bai (2009): unit and time effects
plus \code{r} common factors \code{f_t} with unit loadings \code{lambda_i}, which let
units follow different trends (a difference in differences assumes
\code{r = 0}). On the never-treated units the estimator of Bai (2009)
alternates the factors, \code{sqrt(T)} times the first \code{r} eigenvectors of
\verb{W W'} for the doubly demeaned residuals \code{W} net of covariates
(normalised \verb{F'F / T = I}), and \code{b}, by least squares of the doubly
demeaned outcome less the common component on the doubly demeaned
covariates, until \code{b} changes by less than \code{tol}. Each treated unit's
\code{alpha_i} and \code{lambda_i} come from least squares of its residuals on
\verb{(1, f_t)} over its periods before adoption, which must number more
than \code{r + 1}; its counterfactual \code{y0} is the model's value at every
period, and its effect at a period is \code{y - y0}. \code{att} averages the
effects of the treated periods; \code{eff} averages them by periods since
adoption (event time \code{e}, 0 the first treated period; the effects
before adoption are the fit's in-sample gaps).

\code{r = NULL} chooses the number of factors from 0 to \code{rmax} by the
cross-validation of Xu (2017, sec. 3.1, algorithm 1): each treated
unit's pre-adoption periods are left out one at a time, the loadings
fitted on the rest and the left-out outcome predicted; the \code{r} with the
smallest mean squared prediction error is kept (\code{cv}). The search stops
at the first \code{r} some treated unit has too few periods for.

The standard errors are those of a bootstrap of units, the "simple
nonparametric bootstrap" Xu (2017, sec. 3.2, p. 63) finds valid when
the treated units are many: each draw takes as many units as the panel
has, with replacement, and refits with the chosen \code{r}; draws with no
treated unit or with no more control units than \code{r} fail and are left
out. With few treated units Xu (2017)
prefers a parametric bootstrap that resamples residuals, which is not
built here. Rows of a unit drawn twice enter twice.

Barber (2026) estimates the effect of the New York City congestion
charge on fine particles, traffic speeds and arrests with the
generalized synthetic control, and the spatial shift of arrests with a
difference in differences (see \code{\link[=didfe]{didfe()}}).
